NNAAMMEE
       dpkg-deb - Debian package archive (.deb) manipulation tool

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ddppkkgg--ddeebb [_o_p_t_i_o_n...] _c_o_m_m_a_n_d

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       ddppkkgg--ddeebb packs, unpacks and provides information about Debian archives.

       Use ddppkkgg to install and remove packages from your system.

       You can also invoke ddppkkgg--ddeebb by calling ddppkkgg with whatever options you
       want to pass to ddppkkgg--ddeebb. ddppkkgg will spot that you wanted ddppkkgg--ddeebb and
       run it for you.

       For most commands taking an input archive argument, the archive can be
       read from standard input if the archive name is given as a single minus
       character («--»); otherwise lack of support will be documented in their
       respective command description.

CCOOMMMMAANNDDSS
       --bb, ----bbuuiilldd _b_i_n_a_r_y_-_d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y [_a_r_c_h_i_v_e|_d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y]
           Creates a debian archive from the filesystem tree stored in _b_i_n_a_r_y_-
           _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y. _b_i_n_a_r_y_-_d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y must have a DDEEBBIIAANN subdirectory, which
           contains the control information files such as the control file
           itself. This directory will _n_o_t appear in the binary package's
           filesystem archive, but instead the files in it will be put in the
           binary package's control information area.

           Unless you specify ----nnoocchheecckk, ddppkkgg--ddeebb will read DDEEBBIIAANN//ccoonnttrrooll and
           parse it. It will check the file for syntax errors and other
           problems, and display the name of the binary package being built.
           ddppkkgg--ddeebb will also check the permissions of the maintainer scripts
           and other files found in the DDEEBBIIAANN control information directory.

           If no _a_r_c_h_i_v_e is specified then ddppkkgg--ddeebb will write the package
           into the file _b_i_n_a_r_y_-_d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y..ddeebb.

           If the archive to be created already exists it will be overwritten.

           If the second argument is a directory then ddppkkgg--ddeebb will write to
           the file _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y//_p_a_c_k_a_g_e___v_e_r_s_i_o_n___a_r_c_h..ddeebb..  When a target
           directory is specified, rather than a file, the ----nnoocchheecckk option
           may not be used (since ddppkkgg--ddeebb needs to read and parse the package
           control file to determine which filename to use).

       --II, ----iinnffoo _a_r_c_h_i_v_e [_c_o_n_t_r_o_l_-_f_i_l_e_-_n_a_m_e...]
           Provides information about a binary package archive.

           If no _c_o_n_t_r_o_l_-_f_i_l_e_-_n_a_m_es are specified then it will print a summary
           of the contents of the package as well as its control file.

           If any _c_o_n_t_r_o_l_-_f_i_l_e_-_n_a_m_es are specified then ddppkkgg--ddeebb will print
           them in the order they were specified; if any of the components
           weren't present it will print an error message to stderr about each
           one and exit with status 2.

       --WW, ----sshhooww _a_r_c_h_i_v_e
           Provides information about a binary package archive in the format
           specified by the ----sshhoowwffoorrmmaatt argument. The default format displays
           the package's name and version on one line, separated by a
           tabulator.

       --ff, ----ffiieelldd _a_r_c_h_i_v_e [_c_o_n_t_r_o_l_-_f_i_e_l_d_-_n_a_m_e...]
           Extracts control file information from a binary package archive.

           If no _c_o_n_t_r_o_l_-_f_i_e_l_d_-_n_a_m_es are specified then it will print the
           whole control file.

           If any are specified then ddppkkgg--ddeebb will print their contents, in
           the order in which they appear in the control file. If more than
           one _c_o_n_t_r_o_l_-_f_i_e_l_d_-_n_a_m_e is specified then ddppkkgg--ddeebb will precede each
           with its field name (and a colon and space).

           No errors are reported for fields requested but not found.

       --cc, ----ccoonntteennttss _a_r_c_h_i_v_e
           Lists the contents of the filesystem tree archive portion of the
           package archive. It is currently produced in the format generated
           by ttaarr's verbose listing.

       --xx, ----eexxttrraacctt _a_r_c_h_i_v_e _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y
           Extracts the filesystem tree from a package archive into the
           specified directory.

           Note that extracting a package to the root directory will _n_o_t
           result in a correct installation! Use ddppkkgg to install packages.

           _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y (but not its parents) will be created if necessary, and
           its permissions modified to match the contents of the package.

       --XX, ----vveexxttrraacctt _a_r_c_h_i_v_e _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y
           Is like ----eexxttrraacctt (--xx) with ----vveerrbboossee (--vv) which prints a listing
           of the files extracted as it goes.

       --RR, ----rraaww--eexxttrraacctt _a_r_c_h_i_v_e _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y
           Extracts the filesystem tree from a package archive into a
           specified directory, and the control information files into a
           DDEEBBIIAANN subdirectory of the specified directory (since dpkg 1.16.1).

           The target directory (but not its parents) will be created if
           necessary.

           The input archive is not (currently) processed sequentially, so
           reading it from standard input («--») is nnoott supported.

       ----ccttrrll--ttaarrffiillee _a_r_c_h_i_v_e
           Extracts the control data from a binary package and sends it to
           standard output in ttaarr format (since dpkg 1.17.14). Together with
           ttaarr(1) this can be used to extract a particular control file from a
           package archive.  The input archive will always be processed
           sequentially.

       ----ffssyyss--ttaarrffiillee _a_r_c_h_i_v_e
           Extracts the filesystem tree data from a binary package and sends
           it to standard output in ttaarr format. Together with ttaarr(1) this can
           be used to extract a particular file from a package archive.  The
           input archive will always be processed sequentially.

       --ee, ----ccoonnttrrooll _a_r_c_h_i_v_e [_d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y]
           Extracts the control information files from a package archive into
           the specified directory.

           If no directory is specified then a subdirectory DDEEBBIIAANN in the
           current directory is used.

           The target directory (but not its parents) will be created if
           necessary.

       --??, ----hheellpp
           Show the usage message and exit.

       ----vveerrssiioonn
           Show the version and exit.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       ----sshhoowwffoorrmmaatt==_f_o_r_m_a_t
           This option is used to specify the format of the output ----sshhooww will
           produce. The format is a string that will be output for each
           package listed.

           The string may reference any status field using the “${_f_i_e_l_d_-_n_a_m_e}”
           form, a list of the valid fields can be easily produced using --II on
           the same package. A complete explanation of the formatting options
           (including escape sequences and field tabbing) can be found in the
           explanation of the ----sshhoowwffoorrmmaatt option in ddppkkgg--qquueerryy(1).

           The default for this field is “${Package}\t${Version}\n”.

       --zz_c_o_m_p_r_e_s_s_-_l_e_v_e_l
           Specify which compression level to use on the compressor backend,
           when building a package (default is 9 for gzip, 6 for xz, 3 for
           zstd).  The accepted values are compressor specific.  For gzip,
           from 0-9 with 0 being mapped to compressor none.  For xz from 0-9.
           For zstd from 0-22, with levels from 20 to 22 enabling its ultra
           mode.  Before dpkg 1.16.2 level 0 was equivalent to compressor none
           for all compressors.

       --SS_c_o_m_p_r_e_s_s_-_s_t_r_a_t_e_g_y
           Specify which compression strategy to use on the compressor
           backend, when building a package (since dpkg 1.16.2). Allowed
           values are nnoonnee (since dpkg 1.16.4), ffiilltteerreedd, hhuuffffmmaann, rrllee and
           ffiixxeedd for gzip (since dpkg 1.17.0) and eexxttrreemmee for xz.

       --ZZ_c_o_m_p_r_e_s_s_-_t_y_p_e
           Specify which compression type to use when building a package.
           Allowed values are ggzziipp, xxzz (since dpkg 1.15.6), zzssttdd (since dpkg
           1.21.18) and nnoonnee (default is xxzz).

       ----[[nnoo--]]uunniiffoorrmm--ccoommpprreessssiioonn
           Specify that the same compression parameters should be used for all
           archive members (i.e. ccoonnttrrooll..ttaarr and ddaattaa..ttaarr; since dpkg 1.17.6).
           Otherwise only the ddaattaa..ttaarr member will use those parameters. The
           only supported compression types allowed to be uniformly used are
           nnoonnee, ggzziipp, xxzz and zzssttdd.  The ----nnoo--uunniiffoorrmm--ccoommpprreessssiioonn option
           disables uniform compression (since dpkg 1.19.0).  Uniform
           compression is the default (since dpkg 1.19.0).

       ----tthhrreeaaddss--mmaaxx==_t_h_r_e_a_d_s
           Sets the maximum number of threads allowed for compressors that
           support multi-threaded operations (since dpkg 1.21.9).

       ----rroooott--oowwnneerr--ggrroouupp
           Set the owner and group for each entry in the filesystem tree data
           to root with id 0 (since dpkg 1.19.0).

           NNoottee: This option can be useful for rootless builds (see
           _r_o_o_t_l_e_s_s_-_b_u_i_l_d_s_._t_x_t), but should nnoott be used when the entries have
           an owner or group that is not root.  Support for these will be
           added later in the form of a meta manifest.

       ----ddeebb--ffoorrmmaatt==_f_o_r_m_a_t
           Set the archive format version used when building (since dpkg
           1.17.0).  Allowed values are 22..00 for the new format, and 00..993399000000
           for the old one (default is 22..00).

           The old archive format is less easily parsed by non-Debian tools
           and is now obsolete; its only use is when building packages to be
           parsed by versions of dpkg older than 0.93.76 (September 1995),
           which was released as i386 a.out only.

       ----nnoocchheecckk
           Inhibits ddppkkgg--ddeebb ----bbuuiilldd's usual checks on the proposed contents
           of an archive. You can build any archive you want, no matter how
           broken, this way.

       --vv, ----vveerrbboossee
           Enables verbose output (since dpkg 1.16.1).  This currently only
           affects ----eexxttrraacctt making it behave like ----vveexxttrraacctt.

       --DD, ----ddeebbuugg
           Enables debugging output. This is not very interesting.

