       TTMMPPDDIIRR
           If set, ddppkkgg--ddeebb will use it as the directory in which to create
           temporary files and directories.

       SSOOUURRCCEE__DDAATTEE__EEPPOOCCHH
           If set, it will be used as the timestamp (as seconds since the
           epoch) in the ddeebb(5)'s aarr(5) container and used to clamp the mtime
           in the ttaarr(5) file entries.

NNOOTTEESS
       Do not attempt to use just ddppkkgg--ddeebb to install software! You must use
       ddppkkgg proper to ensure that all the files are correctly placed and the
       package's scripts run and its status and contents recorded.

BBUUGGSS
       ddppkkgg--ddeebb --II _p_a_c_k_a_g_e_1..ddeebb _p_a_c_k_a_g_e_2..ddeebb does the wrong thing.

       There is no authentication on ..ddeebb files; in fact, there isn't even a
       straightforward checksum.  (Higher level tools like APT support
       authenticating ..ddeebb packages retrieved from a given repository, and
       most packages nowadays provide an md5sum control file generated by
       debian/rules. Though this is not directly supported by the lower level
       tools.)

